    "en":  {
               "headline":  "AI chip rout drags ON below its lower band; SiC moat under review",
               "technical":  "Trend: ON closed at $60.84 on 2026-11-20, well below all three EMAs (exponential moving averages, which give more weight to recent prices): EMA50 $73.12, EMA100 $77.14 and EMA200 $77.15. The short average sits under the longer ones, and EMA100 has flattened onto EMA200. That is a classic downtrend. Momentum: MACD (a trend-momentum gauge) is -2.48, below its signal line at -1.85, and the histogram is negative at -0.62, so selling pressure is still building. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) is 34.31, which is weak and close to oversold but not there yet. Position: the price closed just below the lower Bollinger Band at $61.12 (the bands sit about two standard deviations around the 20-day average of $70.10). That signals an overstretched move, which often brings a pause or bounce but does not by itself mean a reversal. The stock is 55% below its 52-week high of $134.92 and 37% above the $44.56 low. Volume was 15.2M, 1.36x the 20-day average, which confirms real selling on the -7.43% day. The nearest support is the $59.14 intraday low; resistance is at $65–66, then around $73.",
               "fundamental":  "Q3 2026, reported 2 Nov, beat onsemi\u0027s own guidance: revenue $1,722M (+11% y/y), non-GAAP gross margin 41.2%, non-GAAP EPS $0.90 and free cash flow $489M. Q4 guidance is $1,715–1,815M in revenue and $0.95–1.07 in non-GAAP EPS. The revenue mix is shifting. AI data center revenue was about $180M (+175% y/y), and the 2030 target was raised to more than $3B. Automotive, the core SiC end market, was flat at $777M. onsemi is buying Ferric (power delivery for AI processors) for $600M plus up to $175M in milestone payments, and Synaptics shareholders approved the cash merger on 17 Nov. The negative: on 6 Nov onsemi cancelled its up-to-$2B Czech SiC expansion, citing changed SiC market economics. For a thesis built on SiC scale, that is a meaningful retreat.",
               "news":  [
                            "2 Nov: Q3 revenue of $1,722M and non-GAAP EPS of $0.90 both beat the midpoint of guidance; Q4 guidance is $1,715–1,815M revenue and $0.95–1.07 EPS. Goldman Sachs called guidance \u0027just above\u0027 expectations and expects the stock to stay \u0027range bound.\u0027",
                            "2 Nov: onsemi agreed to buy Ferric for $600M in cash plus up to $175M in milestone payments and raised its 2030 AI data center revenue target by $500M to more than $3B.",
                            "5 Nov: The PHLX Semiconductor Index fell 12.25%, its second-worst day on record, as investors questioned the AI spending boom. The selloff hit almost every chip stock.",
                            "6 Nov: onsemi and the Czech Ministry of Industry and Trade confirmed that the up-to-$2B Rožnov SiC expansion, announced in June 2024, will not go ahead. Reports cite weak EV demand and cheaper Chinese wafers; no write-down or state-aid repayment has been disclosed yet.",
                            "10 Nov: onsemi launched EliteSiC M4, its first trench-based fourth-generation SiC platform, claiming up to 30% higher power density. The 750 V automotive die is available now, and parts up to 1200 V are due from 2027.",
                            "17 Nov: Synaptics stockholders approved the merger with onsemi with 98.34% of votes cast. The amended deal is about $5.7B in cash ($123 per share), with closing expected in mid-2027.",
                            "After the Q3 report, brokers trimmed targets: Mizuho cut its target from $95 to $87 and BofA from $120 to $100, though the consensus average stays well above the current price.",
                            "20 Nov: ON fell 7.43% during a broad AI/chip rout in which QQQ dropped 5.67% and Nvidia 14%. No onsemi-specific news was found for that day."
                        ],
               "caution":  "The chart is in a confirmed downtrend. The 20 Nov drop came on above-average volume during a sector-wide AI selloff that has shown no sign of ending. A close below the lower Bollinger Band can lead to a bounce, but in strong selloffs prices can keep sliding along the band. On fundamentals, the Rožnov cancellation suggests cheaper Chinese SiC wafers are reducing the value of making wafers in-house. The Synaptics (~$5.7B cash) and Ferric deals add integration and balance-sheet risk, and any Rožnov charge has not yet been disclosed.",
               "opportunity":  "The decline looks mainly sector-driven. Q3 beat guidance, free cash flow was 28% of revenue, and the Q4 guidance midpoint implies sequential growth. AI data center power is the fastest-growing line (+175% y/y), and Ferric adds power delivery right beside the processor, a higher-value spot in the AI rack. EliteSiC M4 keeps onsemi competitive in SiC technology even as it cuts capex. With RSI near 30 and the price at the lower band, the stock is stretched to the downside.",
               "risks":  [
                             "The AI capex selloff could deepen further; the Nasdaq 100 was near a bear market on 20 Nov.",
                             "Cheap Chinese SiC wafers could erode onsemi\u0027s SiC margins and scale advantage.",
                             "Automotive (~45% of Q3 revenue) could stay flat if EV demand remains soft.",
                             "The Synaptics (~$5.7B cash) and Ferric deals carry integration, financing and regulatory-approval risk through mid-2027.",
                             "onsemi has not yet disclosed any Rožnov write-down or repayment of Czech state aid."
